<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ohio</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1803">1803</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ohio is a state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midwestern-united-states">Midwestern United States</a>, between Lake Erie and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio-river">Ohio River</a>. Its land was home to the Erie, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawnee">Shawnee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wyandot">Wyandot</a> and other peoples and was fought over by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, Native confederacies and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in the 18th century. Organised as part of the Northwest Territory, it became the 17th state of the Union in 1803.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ohio.jpg" alt="Ohio" width="320" height="198" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Eisenmann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ohio.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By about 1350 the site was largely abandoned, part of a wider emptying of the central Mississippi and lower <b>Ohio</b> valleys that archaeologists call the Vacant Quarter.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cahokia">Cahokia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>While the British colonies grew to well over a million people by 1760, Canada had about 70,000, and its long line of posts from the St Lawrence to the <b>Ohio</b> and the Mississippi was thinly held.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Grave goods at Mound City include hundreds of pounds of obsidian traceable to Yellowstone, and the <b>Ohio</b> Hopewell may have made pilgrimages there, a walk of about 2,000 miles (3,200 kilometres) each way.</blockquote><a href="/culture/hopewell-culture">Hopewell culture · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…earthworks in the East for thousands of years, at Watson Brake in Louisiana about 3500 BCE and at Poverty Point from about 1700 BCE, and in the <b>Ohio</b> valley the Adena and Hopewell peoples built burial mounds and great geometric enclosures between about 500 BCE and 500 CE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mississippian-culture">Mississippian culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…2006 the term neo-Luddism had emerged for opposition to many forms of technology; a manifesto of the Second Luddite Congress, held at Barnesville, <b>Ohio</b>, in April 1996, called it “a leaderless movement of passive resistance to consumerism and the increasingly bizarre and frightening technologies of the Computer Age”.</blockquote><a href="/movement/luddites">Luddites · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Sojourner Truth, who had freed herself from slavery, spoke at a women’s rights convention in Akron, <b>Ohio</b>, in 1851; the famous version of her speech, with its refrain “Ain’t I a woman?”, was published by Frances Dana Gage only in 1863 and differs from reports made at the…</blockquote><a href="/movement/womens-suffrage-movement">Women's suffrage movement · People</a></li></ul></section>
